%I A400654 #35 Oct 31 2026 15:42:12
%S A400654 1,2,3,9,10,21,27,39,50,58,63,81,117,147,189,243,250,273,290,351,441,
%T A400654 507,567,729,819,1029,1053,1250,1323,1450,1521,1682,1701,1911,2067,
%U A400654 2187,2457,3081,3087,3159,3549,3969,4401,4563,5103,5733,6201,6250,6561,6591
%N A400654 Numbers k such that k divides 17^k + 1.
%C A400654 From _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 19 2026: (Start)
%C A400654 No term is divisible by 4 or by 17, since 17^k + 1 == 2 (mod 16) and 17^k + 1 == 1 (mod 17); so every term is odd or twice an odd number.
%C A400654 If k is a term and p is an odd prime dividing 17^k + 1, then k*p is also a term (by the lifting-the-exponent lemma); for example, 3 -> 3*7 = 21 and 3*13 = 39, 2 -> 2*5 = 10 and 2*29 = 58. Hence the sequence is infinite, and contains 3^j, 2*5^j and 2*29^j for all j >= 0. (End)
%H A400654 Ian Tam, <a href="/A400654/b400654.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2223</a>
%e A400654 3 is a term because 17^3 + 1 = 4914 = 3 * 1638.
%e A400654 9 is a term because 17^9 + 1 = 118587876498 = 9 * 13176430722.
%p A400654 q:= k-> is(0=1+17&^k mod k):
%p A400654 select(q, [$1..20000])[];  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Oct 09 2026
%t A400654 Select[Range[7000], PowerMod[17, #, #] == # - 1 &] (* _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 19 2026 *)
%o A400654 (Python)
%o A400654 def ok(k): return pow(17, k, k) == k - 1
%o A400654 print([k for k in range(1, 6600) if ok(k)]) # _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 19 2026
